d F. Lev\nDate: Wed Oct  7 13:34:53 2020\n\nWe give a comprehensive description of the sets $A$ in finite cyclic groups such that $|2A|<\\frac94|A|$; namely, we show that any set with this property is densely contained in a (one-dimensional) coset progression. This improves earlier results of Deshouillers-Freiman and Balasubramanian-Pandey.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSmall doubling in cyclic groups - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0022314X22001408)\nciteturn3search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; Date: February 2023 ... Small doubling in cyclic groups ... One of the central problems of additive combinatorics is to understand the structure of small-doubling sets, or approximate subgroups, which are sets A of group elements such that the sumset has size comparable with the size of A.\n\nWe use the additive notation throughout since we will be concerned with abelian groups only, and particularly with the finite cyclic groups, which we denote {\\mathbb{Z}}_{n}; here n is the order of the group. ... In Section 5 we establish a number of properties of the sets with a “very small” doubling coefficients, including the asymmetric case. ... Lev et al.\n\n## Journal of Number Theory\n\nDate: February 2023\n\nPages: 561-614\n\nVolume: Volume 243\n\n## Published by: Elsevier\n\n### Published by\n\nShow more\n\nResearch article\n\nGet rights and content\n\n# \n\nGeneral Section\n\nSmall doubling in cyclic groups\n\nAuthor links open overlay panelVsevolod F. Lev\n\nShow more\n\nCite\n\nAdd to Mendeley\n\nShare\n\n10.1016/j.jnt.2022.06.001\n\nAccess through your organizationPurchase PDF\n\nMore actions\n\n  * Article preview\n  * Cited by (2)\n  * Metrics\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe give a comprehensive description of the sets A in finite cyclic groups such that ; namely, we show that any set with this property is densely contained in a (one-dimensional) coset progression. This improves earlier results of Deshouillers-Freiman and Balasubramanian-Pandey.\n\n## MSC\n\nprimary,\n\n11P70\n\n;\n\nsecondary,\n\n11B75\n\n## Keywords\n\nSmall doubling\n\n;\n\nDoubling constant\n\n;\n\nCoset progressions\n\n  * Previous article in this issue\n  * Next article in this issue\n\n## Introduction\n\nOne of the central problems of additive combinatorics is to understand the structure of small-doubling sets, or approximate subgroups, which are sets A of group elements such that the sumset has size comparable with the size of A.\n\nWe use the additive notation throughout since we will be concerned with abelian groups only, and particularly with the finite cyclic groups, which we denote {\\mathbb{Z}}_{n}; here n is the order of the group. Our goal is to prove the following result.\n\nTheorem 1.1\n\nLet n be a positive integer. If a set satisfies , then one of the following holds:\n\n  * (i)\n\nThere is a subgroup such that A is contained in an H-coset and , where .\n\n  * (ii)\n\nThere is a proper subgroup and an arithmetic progression P of size such that , , and\n\n  * (iii)\n\nThere is a proper subgroup such that A meets exactly three H-cosets, the cosets are not in an arithmetic progression, and\n\nWe notice that the coefficient \\frac{9}{4} in Theorem 1.1 is in fact a threshold in the sense that the assumption cannot be relaxed even to : for instance, if n is large enough, and with and , then while A does not have the structure described in Theorem 1.1.\n\nOur argument follows the general line of reasoning introduced by Freiman in [F61] and then pursued by other authors; namely, we use character sums to conclude that small doubling leads to a biased distribution, and then use the bias as a starting point for the combinatorial part of the proof. The improvements come from a refinement in the character sums component, in the spirit of [LS20]; from replacing the main auxiliary result used in Deshouillers-Freiman [DF03, Theorem 2] with its stronger version [L22, Theorem 2], see Section 3; and, finally, from using an intricate combinatorial analysis.\n\nThe rest of the paper is structured as follows. In the next section we introduce the notation that will be used throughout and considered standard. In Section 3 we prove Theorem 1.3 in the special case where the image of the small-doubling set under a suitable homomorphism is rectifiable; although this case is of principal importance, the proof is, essentially, just a reduction to [L22, Theorem 2]. In Section 4 we present Kneser's theorem and a relaxed version of Kemperman's theorem. In Section 5 we establish a number of properties of the sets with a “very small” doubling coefficients, including the asymmetric case. Some other general results on set addition in abelian groups, mostly of combinatorial nature, are gathered in Section 6. Section 7 establishes a number of results about the minimal counterexample set (which, as we eventually show, does not exist). Two more results of this sort, Lemma 8.1, Lemma 9.1, show that the minimum counterexample set, if it exists, meets at least four cosets of any subgroup, with the obvious exceptions; these two lemmas are singled out into dedicated Sections 8 and 9. Their proofs are quite technical and some readers may prefer to skip the details and proceed to Section 10 where the character sum component of the argument is presented. The proof is completed in the concluding Section 11.\n\n## Acknowledgment\n\nI am grateful to David Grynkiewicz for his interest and useful conversations, and to the referee for a very careful reading of the manuscript and a number of truly valuable remarks and suggestions.\n\n## References (16)\n\n  * R. Balasubramanian et al.\n\n### On a theorem of Deshouillers and Freiman\n\n### Eur. J. Comb.\n\n(2018)\n\n  * V.F. Lev et al.\n\n### Small doubling in prime-order groups: from 2.4 to 2.6\n\n### J. Number Theory\n\n(2020)\n\n  * J.E. Olson\n\n### On the sum of two sets in a group\n\n### J. Number Theory\n\n(1984)\